{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2131201979/16/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"db695cfb\" lines=\"8-19\">\n<description>\nConnect same-color diagonal pixel pairs, then emit perpendicular rays from differently-colored pixels on those diagonals.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Line color (e.g. blue) has pixel pairs aligned on shared diagonals. Trigger color (e.g. magenta) pixels sit along those diagonals.\n\nRule:\n- Draw diagonal lines connecting each pair of same-color aligned pixels.\n- Where the diagonal crosses a trigger pixel, emit perpendicular diagonal rays (in the trigger's color) extending to the grid boundary.\n\nKey insight: Trigger pixels act as beam-splitters — the perpendicular rays rotate 90° from the connecting diagonal's orientation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"981add89\" lines=\"10-29\">\n<description>\nExtend each top-row control pixel downward as a vertical toggle ray in its own color.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Non-background pixels on the top row are control pixels, each identifying\n  one column and one ray color.\n- Foreground regions below may intersect these control columns.\n\nRule:\n- For every top-row control pixel, process the cells below it in the same\n  column down to the bottom edge.\n- A cell that already has the control color is erased to the background.\n- Every other cell on that downward ray is painted with the control color.\n- The top-row control pixels themselves remain unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\nThe top row is a set of vertical ray launchers; each ray toggles only cells\nmatching its own color and overwrites all other colors along that column.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f341894c\" lines=\"63-78\">\n<description>\nReorient each two-pixel blue/pink segment so it reads pink then blue when viewed from its border marker.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is dominant, with one border-only marker color and blue/pink foreground segments.\n- Every marker lies on the outer border and points inward along its row or column.\n- Along exactly one inward ray from each marker is a single adjacent two-pixel blue/pink segment.\n\nRule\n- For each marker, look inward perpendicular to the border side containing the marker.\n- Rewrite the two blue/pink pixels on that ray so the nearer one is pink and the farther one is blue.\n- Keep marker pixels, background pixels, and all other pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker gives a local viewing direction; the segment's required order is relative to that marker, not to global row or column order.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"494ef9d7\" lines=\"16-30\">\n<description>\nSlide each blue/teal or yellow/orange row pair together while preserving its left-to-right order.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a black background.\n- Each nonempty row contains exactly two distinct foreground pixels.\n- Blue/teal and yellow/orange row pairs are the moving pairs; other color pairs are distractors.\n\nRule\n- For each moving pair, keep the left pixel fixed and move the right pixel left until it touches the left pixel.\n- Clear the moved pixel's original location, and leave non-moving rows unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- Only same-hue-family pixel pairs attract horizontally; separated unrelated colors do not compact.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"52364a65\" lines=\"10-24\">\n<description>\nErase the two leftmost columns of every foreground object, leaving the rest of each object in place.\n\nInput structure:\n- A grid has one dominant background color and several monochromatic 4-connected foreground objects.\n- Each foreground object spans at least two columns in its bounding box.\n\nRule:\n- Find each same-color 4-connected foreground object.\n- Within each object's own bounding box, set foreground pixels in the two leftmost columns to the background color.\n- Leave all other pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- The deletion is measured from each object's local left edge, not from the grid edge or from a shared global column.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"83eb0a57\" lines=\"72-86\">\n<description>\nCrop the largest object and overlay each smaller sprite where its shared marker pattern matches.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the most common color, and the foreground has one largest destination object plus smaller source sprites.\n- Each source sprite shares exactly one marker color with the destination object.\n\nRule\n- Output the tight bbox crop of the largest object.\n- Translate each source sprite so every connected marker component of the shared color exactly matches destination marker components.\n- Paste source sprites in decreasing area order, so smaller sprites can sit on top of larger ones when they overlap.\n\nKey insight\n- The shared-color marker components are alignment keys; matching whole marker components distinguishes singleton slots from larger same-color marker blocks.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"2ba387bc\" lines=\"6-21\">\n<description>\nSort scattered 4×4 squares into two columns by type: frames left, solids right.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Multiple 4×4 objects on bg.\n- Frames: colored 1px border, bg-filled 2×2 interior.\n- Solids: entirely filled with one non-bg color.\n\nRule:\n- Classify each 4×4 object as frame or solid by its interior.\n- Sort each group top-to-bottom by input row.\n- Stack frames in left column (cols 0–3), solids in right (cols 4–7).\n- Output is 8 wide; height = 4 × max(#frames, #solids), bg-padded.\n\nKey insight: Frame vs solid is determined solely by whether the 2×2 interior matches bg.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6cbe9eb8\" lines=\"79-94\">\n<description>\nRebuild the foreground rectangles as a largest-to-smallest lower-left nesting.\n\nInput structure\n- A periodic wallpaper background is overlaid by foreground colors that each form a rectangular frame or solid rectangle.\n- The largest foreground rectangle is the output frame; smaller foreground rectangles are the objects to nest inside it.\n- A frame may be partially hidden by a smaller rectangle, but its visible pixels still determine its bbox.\n\nRule\n- Ignore the wallpaper and crop the result to the largest foreground rectangle's bbox size.\n- Sort foreground rectangles by bbox area from largest to smallest.\n- Reconstruct each frame or solid rectangle, placing every next smaller rectangle in the lower-left of the current rectangle's usable region.\n\nKey insight\n- The scattered background colors are not objects; the meaningful order is the unique descending rectangle size chain, and each step nests into the previous rectangle's lower-left interior.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f8f52ecc\" lines=\"107-122\">\n<description>\nConnect same-colored foreground components with one-cell-wide orthogonal paths around teal wall segments.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- Teal cells are fixed wall segments and are never source connectors.\n- Other foreground colors appear as one or more 4-connected components; colors with a single component stay unchanged.\n\nRule\n- For each non-background, non-teal color, read its 4-connected components in row-major order.\n- Connect each consecutive pair by the shortest clear L-shaped path between the two components.\n- Clear paths may pass only through background or the same color, and they preserve the previous travel axis when both bends are possible.\n\nKey insight\n- The output is not a global recolor: each color traces a routed chain through its own components while teal cells and other colors act as blockers.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"97c75046\" lines=\"88-107\">\n<description>\nMove the single marker to the endpoint reached by sliding along the obstacle contour.\n\nInput structure:\n- A dominant background color fills the canvas.\n- One single-pixel marker sits away from a connected obstacle region of the\n  remaining color.\n- The marker has exactly one horizontal or vertical sightline to the obstacle.\n\nRule:\n- Find the last background pixel before the obstacle along that sightline.\n- From that pixel, try the two diagonal turns that continue around the\n  obstacle boundary.\n- Move the marker to the endpoint of the turn that remains 4-adjacent to the\n  obstacle for the longest distance.\n\nKey insight:\nThe marker is not placed by distance to the obstacle; it first points to the\nobstacle, then follows the visible outside contour until that contour segment ends.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"581f7754\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nSlide multi-color sprites to align their marker pixels with matching isolated border anchors.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Multi-color sprites in the interior; each has a minority-color \"marker\" pixel.\n- Isolated border pixels (non-bg with all 4-neighbors bg) act as anchors.\n\nRule:\n- Match each sprite to the border anchor sharing its marker color.\n- Top/bottom-edge anchor (non-corner): slide sprite horizontally so marker column = anchor column.\n- Left/right-edge anchor (or corner): slide sprite vertically so marker row = anchor row.\n\nKey insight: The anchor's edge position determines the slide axis — top/bottom means horizontal, left/right (or corner) means vertical.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"42f14c03\" lines=\"75-83\">\n<description>\nCrop the foreground object whose missing cells match marker shapes, and fill those missing cells with the marker colors.\n\nInput structure\nA dominant background surrounds several monochromatic foreground objects. Exactly one object has background gaps inside its bounding box whose component shapes are matched by separate marker objects.\nRule\nFind that object, crop its bounding box, and replace each background gap component with the color of the separate marker component or components having the same normalized shape.\nKey insight\nThe marker objects do not mark absolute positions; their shapes identify which gaps in the selected object should be colored, while objects with unmatched gaps are distractors.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"cbebaa4b\" lines=\"51-65\">\n<description>\nAssemble scattered jigsaw sprites around a central yellow anchor by pairing red connector pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = black (0). Several colored sprites scattered on the grid.\n- One anchor sprite contains a 3×3 yellow (4) block.\n- Each sprite has red (2) connector pixels along its edges.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
